 "field" "answer" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "local_authored" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/repairs.json" "file_sha256" "0328005daa91919dd8bb81fd729228440954ec812e808e91c99da77efc57aee9" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "571ee7e4f8997fdfae74f800edd84d0efce760607b5043aa85a8d9d81962095f" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-118780" "field" "answer" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "local_authored" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/repairs.json" "file_sha256" "0328005daa91919dd8bb81fd729228440954ec812e808e91c99da77efc57aee9" "transaction" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/transaction.edn" "transaction_sha256" "571ee7e4f8997fdfae74f800edd84d0efce760607b5043aa85a8d9d81962095f" "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/format-audit/database-repair/verification.json" "basis" 653} {"question" "q-118779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $y\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}}+3x\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{3}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{3}}={x}^{8}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{6}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{6}},$ which has order $6.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $6.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "3"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "4"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "12cc794d0be2ef88bc935bf87f761f03ca15512d817ed3733ccab3d5ee5c2f7e" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "I, II, and III"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer/value" "value" [:answer.type/text "III only"] "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/state.json" "file_sha256" "c3e70e5853836023f0af7fa2086eefc9e059096b42b65cfb5126b52e964980a4" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8459.json" "file_sha256" "296788228679c99975543037b8c07a110abb9645b65095ff5a1deb403a020e7b" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8459.json" "file_sha256" "296788228679c99975543037b8c07a110abb9645b65095ff5a1deb403a020e7b" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8460.json" "file_sha256" "b5afa99cecbe66f86cb0d15aae4e9cad338835a33489e7cbad2da8a3786c42b6" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8460.json" "file_sha256" "b5afa99cecbe66f86cb0d15aae4e9cad338835a33489e7cbad2da8a3786c42b6" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8461.json" "file_sha256" "6834e5536311eec63e9df52f7815519de9d7b0327f9535b2feb28e87a2344b60" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "e-8461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/example-8461.json" "file_sha256" "6834e5536311eec63e9df52f7815519de9d7b0327f9535b2feb28e87a2344b60" "basis" 870 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13980600/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$\n- $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4f0bcbf286cf980ec89f82207c3a4d7153f6f018769cfca8c61ab160afecc43" "basis" 1136 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14008751/state.json" "file_sha256" "c4f0bcbf286cf980ec89f82207c3a4d7153f6f