/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118779" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation ${x}^{2}\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{7}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{7}}-9{x}^{7}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=2\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}}?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\frac{{\\text{d}}^{8}y}{\\text{d}{x}^{8}},$ which has order $8.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $8.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118780" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-118780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "2"] ["math" "6"] ["math" "7"] ["math" "8"] ["math" "9"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118780" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y$\n- $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96705" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-96705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96705" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}-3{x}^{2}=1$\n- ${y}^{′}+6y={e}^{3y}$\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2{x}^{2}+1)$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-3{x}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =3{x}^{2}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x)=3{x}^{2}+1.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}+6y & ={e}^{3y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={e}^{3y}-6y \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)={e}^{3y}-6y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118859" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-118859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118859" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y}=0$\n- $\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy={e}^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{y} & =0 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{y} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{y}.$\n\n- Equation III is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′}-xy & ={e}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{y}^{′} & =xy+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =2(xy+{e}^{x}) \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=2(xy+{e}^{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96387" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-96387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96387" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-95984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={e}^{y}$\n- $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y=0$\n- $2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy=\\sqrt{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-95984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)={e}^{y}.$\n\n- Equation II is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3y & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3y.$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-xy & =\\sqrt{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{1}{2}(xy+\\sqrt{x}).$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-95984" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-95984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-95984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-95984" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\n- ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\n- ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is *not* an autonomous differential equation. It is not of the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation II is *not* an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y & ={e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & ={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x} \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)={x}^{2}y+{e}^{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form ${y}^{′}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1 \\\\ {y}^{′} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}+1.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96711" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\n- $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x}$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System III is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(4)=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118791" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y,\\,y(-1)=3$\n- $8{y}^{′}-xy=3$\n- $3{y}^{″}=4y,\\,y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $(1+{x}^{2}){y}^{′}=8y$ together with the initial condition $y(-1)=3.$\n\n- System II is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $3{y}^{″}=4y$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(-1)=0,\\,{y}^{′}(-1)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118786" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-118786" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118786" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I only"] ["text" "I, II, and III"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118786" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\n- $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\n- ${y}^{′}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{′}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{′}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96992" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-96992" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96992" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96992" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "I and II only"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $2x-{y}^{′}=\\frac{1}{y}$\n- $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2},\\,y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1$\n- $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′},\\,y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{y}^{′}-2{e}^{x}y={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(\\operatorname{ln}⁡2)=1.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2x{y}^{″}-\\frac{1}{x}={y}^{′}$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=1,\\,{y}^{′}(2)=1.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-96988" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y,\\,y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4$\n- $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(0)=2$\n- $6{y}^{″}+xy={e}^{x}$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *third*-order differential equation ${x}^{2}{y}^{‴}+9{x}^{3}{y}^{′}=-7y$ together with *three* initial conditions $y(0)={y}^{′}(0)={y}^{″}(0)=-4.$ This is a third-order initial value problem.\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=2.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. We have here a second-order ordinary differential equation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118788" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "16df356ef31cec545731e1f9acaea88a42f029527110ece34371c9c2bae4b2cc"} {"question" "q-118788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and II only"] ["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"] ["text" "None of those listed"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "q-118788" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/state.json" "file_sha256" "10bda9637715baf9cdfb337ccfa9d74b08b2b7bcef1833d0feb2b5c50a042eee"} {"question" "e-8459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "What is the order of the ordinary differential equation $2x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3}+y^2\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^2y}{\\textrm{d}x^2}=3x^2?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/example-8459.json" "file_sha256" "296788228679c99975543037b8c07a110abb9645b65095ff5a1deb403a020e7b"} {"question" "e-8459" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The order of an ordinary differential equation (ODE) is the order of the highest derivative in the equation.\n\nThe highest derivative in the given ODE is $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}^3y}{\\textrm{d}x^3},$ which has order $3.$\n\nTherefore, the order of the ODE is $3.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/example-8459.json" "file_sha256" "296788228679c99975543037b8c07a110abb9645b65095ff5a1deb403a020e7b"} {"question" "e-8460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\n- $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/example-8460.json" "file_sha256" "b5afa99cecbe66f86cb0d15aae4e9cad338835a33489e7cbad2da8a3786c42b6"} {"question" "e-8460" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, $y'=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. We see that it is of the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ where $f(y)= y^3.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-{y}^{3} & =2 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{{y}^{3}+2}{x} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)= \\dfrac{y^3+2}{x}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y} & =1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =1+3\\sqrt{y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)= 1+3\\sqrt{y}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/example-8460.json" "file_sha256" "b5afa99cecbe66f86cb0d15aae4e9cad338835a33489e7cbad2da8a3786c42b6"} {"question" "e-8461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\n- $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\n- $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\n- $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/example-8461.json" "file_sha256" "6834e5536311eec63e9df52f7815519de9d7b0327f9535b2feb28e87a2344b60"} {"question" "e-8461" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\ny' = f(x,y), \\quad y(x_0) = y_0,\n$$\n\nwhere $x_0$ and $y_0$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^2-3y' = y^3$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=6.$\n\n- System II is also a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}$ together with the initial condition $y(2)=e.$\n\n- System III is not a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a *second*-order differential equation $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y'$ together with *two* initial conditions $y(2)=3,\\, y'(2) = 2.$ This is a second-order initial value problem.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14040896/example-8461.json" "file_sha256" "6834e5536311eec63e9df52f7815519de9d7b0327f9535b2feb28e87a2344b60"}] "usage" {"q-118780" [] "q-96387" [] "q-96705" [] "q-95984" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186282816} {"kind" "responses" "entity" 17592186282816}] "q-96992" [] "q-96669" [] "e-8459" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186282702}] "q-95983" [] "q-118791" [] "q-118786" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186282960} {"kind" "responses" "entity" 17592186282960}] "q-96988" [] "e-8461" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186282958}] "e-8460" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186282706}] "q-96711" [{"kind" "presentations" "entity" 17592186282818} {"kind" "responses" "entity" 17592186282818}] "q-118779" []} "basis" 1474 "retractions" [[17592186167378 :question/problem "Which of the following are third-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. $y^{\\prime \\prime \\prime } + 3xy^{\\prime } = xy$\nII. $3y^{\\prime \\prime }y^{\\prime } - 2xy = 2y^{\\prime }$\nIII. $x^{3}y^{\\prime } - 2x = y^{\\prime \\prime }$"] [17592186167378 :question/worked-solution "The order is the highest derivative in each equation. Equation I contains $y'''$ and is third order. Equations II and III contain $y''$ but no third derivative, so both are second order. Select **I only**."] [17592186167378 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186262013 :question/problem "Which of the following are first-order ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. ${y}^{‴}+(\\operatorname{sin}x){y}^{'}={x}^{2}y$\nII. $5{y}^{''}{y}^{'}+3xy=4{y}^{'}$\nIII. ${({y}^{'})}^{2}-2{x}^{2}=y$"] [17592186262013 :question/worked-solution "A differential equation is of first-order if the largest derivative term in the equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nKeeping this in mind, let's check each given equation.\n\n- Equation I is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{‴},$ so this is a third-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation II is not a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{''},$ so this is a second-order differential equation.\n\n- Equation III is a first-order differential equation. The highest derivative in this equation is ${y}^{'}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""] [17592186262013 :question/answer-fields 17592186262014] [17592186257268 :question/answer-fields 17592186274341] [17592186262063 :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. ${e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3}=0$\nII. $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x=\\operatorname{ln}y$\nIII. $5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x}=0$"] [17592186262063 :question/worked-solution "An ordinary differential equation with dependent variable $y$ and independent variable $x$ is said to be autonomous if it can be written in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ or equivalently, ${y}^{'}=f(y).$\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each of the differential equations:\n\n- Equation I is an autonomous differential equation. Writing the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{y}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-3{y}^{3} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(y)=3{y}^{3}{e}^{-y}.$\n\n- Equation II is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2x & =\\operatorname{ln}y \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\operatorname{ln}y+2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}y+2x.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\n- Equation III is not an autonomous differential equation. If we isolate the derivative term, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5x\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-\\frac{y}{x} & =0 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =f(x,y)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $f(x,y)=\\frac{y}{5{x}^{2}}.$ So, it is not possible to write the equation in the form $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=f(y).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""] [17592186262063 :question/answer-fields 17592186262064] [17592186167405 :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. $y^{\\prime } = e^{x}$\nII. $y^{\\prime } - \\frac{3}{y} = 0$\nIII. $\\frac{1}{2}y^{\\prime } - xy = e^{x}$"] [17592186167405 :question/worked-solution "Isolate $y'$ in each equation. I gives $y'=e^x$ and III gives $y'=2xy+2e^x$, so both depend explicitly on $x$. II gives $y'=3/y$, depending only on $y$. Therefore the equations that are **not** autonomous are **I and III only**."] [17592186167405 :question/difficulty 17592186045463] [17592186167379 :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. $\\frac{dy}{dx} = e^{y}$\nII. $\\frac{dy}{dx} - 3y = 0$\nIII. $2\\frac{dy}{dx} - xy = \\sqrt{x}$"] [17592186167379 :question/worked-solution "An autonomous first-order ODE can be written as $y'=f(y)$ with no explicit $x$ dependence.\n\n- I is already $y'=e^y$.\n- II becomes $y'=3y$.\n- III becomes $y'=(xy+\\sqrt x)/2$, which depends explicitly on $x$.\n\nSelect **I and II only**."] [17592186281047 :question/problem "Which of the following are **not** autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{y}{x})$\nII. ${y}^{′}-{x}^{2}y={e}^{x}$\nIII. ${y}^{′}-\\frac{3}{{y}^{2}}=1$"] [17592186281012 :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. ${y}^{‴}+{y}^{″}+{y}^{′}+y=0,\\,y(1)={y}^{′}(1)={y}^{″}(1)=2$\nII. ${y}^{′}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+y={3}^{x},\\,y(1)=6$\nIII. $\\sqrt{x}{y}^{′}+{e}^{x}y=6,\\,y(4)=-3$"] [17592186262093 :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2},\\,y(0)=1$\nII. $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y,\\,y(1)=2$\nIII. ${y}^{'}-x\\sqrt{y}={e}^{x}$"] [17592186262093 :question/worked-solution "A first-order initial value problem (or IVP) is a first-order ordinary differential equation together with an initial condition that specifies a value of the unknown function at a point in its domain. A first-order initial value problem typically takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}^{'}=f(x,y),\\,y({x}_{0})={y}_{0},\n$$\n\nwhere ${x}_{0}$ and ${y}_{0}$ are constants.\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each option.\n\n- System I is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $3{x}^{2}{y}^{'}={y}^{2}$ together with the initial condition $y(0)=1.$\n\n- System II is a first-order initial value problem. It consists of a first-order differential equation $\\frac{{y}^{'}}{2}-{x}^{2}{y}^{2}=y$ together with the initial condition $y(1)=2.$\n\n- System III  is not a first-order initial value problem. We have a first-order ordinary differential equation, but there is no initial condition.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""] [17592186262093 :question/answer-fields 17592186262094] [17592186166584 :question/problem "Which of the following are autonomous ordinary differential equations?\n\nI. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=y^3$\n\nII. $x\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}- y^3=2$\n\nIII. $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}-3\\sqrt{y}=1$"] [17592186166585 :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a first-order initial value problem?\n\nI. $x^2-3y' = y^3, \\quad y(1)=6$\n\nII. $x^3y'-e^x= \\sqrt{y}, \\quad y(2)=e$\n\nIII. $2y'' - \\dfrac{x}{2} = 3y', \\quad y(2)=3, \\quad y'(2) = 2$"]] "immutable_answers" [17592186165817 17592186165818 17592186165819 17592186165820 17592186165821 17592186165823 17592186165824 17592186165825 17592186165826 17592186165827 17592186165979 17592186165980 17592186165981 17592186165982 17592186165983 17592186262015 17592186262016 17592186262017 17592186262018 17592186262019 17592186262020 17592186262021 17592186262022 17592186262065 17592186262066 17592186262067 17592186262068 17592186262069 17592186262070 17592186262071 17592186262072 17592186262095 17592186262096 17592186262097 17592186262098 17592186262099 17592186262100 17592186262101 17592186262102 17592186270327 17592186270328 17592186270329 17592186270330 17592186270331 17592186280986 17592186280987 17592186280988 17592186280989 17592186280990 17592186281014 17592186281015 17592186281016 17592186281017 17592186281018 17592186281049 17592186281050 17592186281051 17592186281052 17592186281053 17592186288797 17592186288798 17592186288799 17592186288800 17592186288801 17592186294498 17592186294499 17592186294500 17592186294501 17592186294502] "immutable_fields" [[17592186165816 :answer-field/correct] [17592186165816 :answer-field/id] [17592186165816 :answer-field/key] [17592186165816 :answer-field/type] [17592186165822 :answer-field/correct] [17592186165822 :answer-field/id] [17592186165822 :answer-field/key] [17592186165822 :answer-field/type] [17592186165978 :answer-field/correct] [17592186165978 :answer-field/id] [17592186165978 :an